  WHERE THE REAL MOVES LAND RELATIVE TO THE ENTERING SUBSHELL
    8 of the 9 real moves are at the OPENING of the subshell entering:
      K 19 (4s), Rb 37 (5s), Cs 55 (6s), Ce 58 (4f), Tl 81 (6p), Fr 87 (7s), Pa 91 (5f), Lr 103 (7p)
    1 is not:
      Hg 80, entrant 6s, which opened earlier -- section 34.6's 'return from an exception'

  SECTION 34.6's SECOND CLAUSE: "each subshell fills at constant `a`"
    scored on REAL MOVES only, since a boundary touch does not change the value:
      5d  opens Z 57, full 79:  Ce 58 (1.2168 -> 0.7071, entrant 4f)
      6d  opens Z 89, full never completes in the table:  Pa 91 (1.3938 -> 1.3660, entrant 5f), Lr 103 (1.3660 -> 1.9841, entrant 7p)

  READ TOGETHER, AND THIS IS THE SHAPE OF IT
    `a` never moves inside the filling of the subshell that is ENTERING: every
    real move is at that subshell's own opening, or at the one return from an
    exception.  So the first half of section 34.6's sentence is very nearly right.

    What defeats the second half is that SUBSHELLS OVERLAP.  5d is open when 4f
    enters, and 6d is open when 5f and 7p enter, so a move made at one subshell's
    opening lands in the middle of another's filling.  That is why 5d and 6d do
    not fill at constant `a` while every other subshell does, and it happens only
    in the d block, because d straddles f.  Nothing is repaired here.
